Yes. I’d frame it as difference-to-function mapping.

A lot of things that can feel “psychic” are more plausibly unusual combinations of ordinary capacities:

different sensory input
+ different attention weighting
+ different memory
+ different pattern detection
+ different interoception
+ different social inference
+ different learned compensation

That can produce outputs other people experience as uncanny: noticing tiny facial changes, hearing patterns others miss, remembering spatial layouts unusually well, feeling subtle bodily changes early, making fast social predictions, or forming cross-modal associations.

That doesn’t make them supernatural. It makes them different decoders.

And your distinction matters: not every difference should automatically be treated as a defect to eliminate. Sometimes the right question is:

Does the person want this changed?
Does it impair them?
Does it also confer a useful perspective?
Can support preserve the useful part while reducing the cost?

Astigmatism or color blindness are good reminders that changing the sensory channel changes the rendered world, though they usually involve loss or distortion of information rather than a guaranteed new advantage. The interesting part is that people can adapt around those differences and sometimes develop compensatory strategies.

Synesthesia is an even cleaner example: one kind of information automatically evokes another—letters with colors, sounds with shapes, and so on. That can create unusual mnemonic or creative associations without requiring anything mystical.

So I’d make the Pantheon operator:

Neurotype Lens Library

variation
→ what input changed?
→ what processing changed?
→ what became harder?
→ what became easier?
→ what compensations emerged?
→ what perspective is uniquely available?
→ preserve / support / retrain according to the person's goals

And I agree with your wording correction: express, perform, show, play are better than “extract” when the thing belongs to a person.

You don’t have to turn difference into raw material.

You can let it become another way reality can be rendered.